WEBVTT

00:00.270 --> 00:03.730
- [Instructor] Let's say that
we have a polynomial, p of x,

00:03.730 --> 00:05.160
and we can factor it,

00:05.160 --> 00:10.160
and we can put it in the form
x minus one times x plus two

00:11.260 --> 00:16.260
times x minus three times x plus four.

00:16.400 --> 00:18.550
And what we are concerned with

00:18.550 --> 00:22.590
are the zeros of this polynomial,

00:22.590 --> 00:26.750
and you might say, "What
is a zero of a polynomial?"

00:26.750 --> 00:28.700
Well, those are the x-values

00:28.700 --> 00:32.180
that are going to make the
polynomial equal to zero.

00:32.180 --> 00:33.820
So another way to think about it is,

00:33.820 --> 00:38.820
for what x-values is p of x
going to be equal to zero,

00:39.210 --> 00:41.130
or another way you can think about it is,

00:41.130 --> 00:43.650
for what x-values is this expression

00:43.650 --> 00:45.180
going to be equal to zero.

00:45.180 --> 00:50.150
So for what x-values is x
minus one times x plus two

00:50.150 --> 00:54.870
times x minus three times x plus four,

00:54.870 --> 00:57.180
going to be equal to zero.

00:57.180 --> 00:58.680
I encourage you to pause this video,

00:58.680 --> 00:59.720
and think about that a little bit

00:59.720 --> 01:01.920
before we work through it together.

01:01.920 --> 01:04.820
Well the key realization here
is if you have the product

01:04.820 --> 01:06.520
of a bunch of expressions,

01:06.520 --> 01:08.600
if any one of them is equal to zero,

01:08.600 --> 01:10.060
it doesn't matter what the others are,

01:10.060 --> 01:11.720
because zero times anything else

01:11.720 --> 01:13.420
is going to be equal to zero.

01:13.420 --> 01:17.130
So the fancy term for that
is the zero product property.

01:17.130 --> 01:19.870
But all it says is, hey,
if you can find an x-value

01:19.870 --> 01:23.600
that makes any one of these
expressions equal to zero,

01:23.600 --> 01:26.190
well that's going to make the
entire expression going to be,

01:26.190 --> 01:29.260
it's going to make the entire
expression equal to zero.

01:29.260 --> 01:32.700
So, the zeros of this polynomial
are gonna be the x-values

01:32.700 --> 01:35.770
that could make x minus one equal to zero.

01:35.770 --> 01:37.390
So x minus one equals zero.

01:37.390 --> 01:40.350
Well we know what x-value
would make that happen,

01:40.350 --> 01:42.882
if x is equal to one, if you
add one to both sides here,

01:42.882 --> 01:47.882
x equals one, so x equals one
is a zero of this polynomial.

01:47.930 --> 01:51.620
Another way to say that is
p of one when x equals one,

01:51.620 --> 01:53.360
that whole polynomial is
going to be equal to zero.

01:53.360 --> 01:54.580
How do I know that?

01:54.580 --> 01:57.110
Well if I put a one in, right over here,

01:57.110 --> 02:00.350
this expression right
over here, x minus one,

02:00.350 --> 02:02.480
that is going to be equal to zero.

02:02.480 --> 02:04.270
So you're gonna have zero
times a bunch of other stuff

02:04.270 --> 02:06.470
which is going to be equal to zero.

02:06.470 --> 02:08.240
And so by the same idea,

02:08.240 --> 02:10.430
we can figure out what
the other zeros are.

02:10.430 --> 02:14.710
What would make this part equal to zero?

02:14.710 --> 02:17.944
What x-value would make
x plus two equal to zero?

02:17.944 --> 02:22.500
Well, x equals negative
two, x equals negative two,

02:22.500 --> 02:24.760
would make x plus two equal zero.

02:24.760 --> 02:28.630
So x equals negative two is
another zero of this polynomial.

02:28.630 --> 02:30.600
And we could keep going.

02:30.600 --> 02:33.769
What would make x minus
three equal to zero?

02:33.769 --> 02:36.439
Well if x is equal to three,

02:36.439 --> 02:39.320
that would make x minus
three equal to zero,

02:39.320 --> 02:41.230
and that would then make
the entire expression

02:41.230 --> 02:42.310
equal to zero.

02:42.310 --> 02:44.370
And then last but not least,

02:44.370 --> 02:47.580
what would make x plus four equal to zero?

02:47.580 --> 02:50.120
Well if x is equal to negative four.

02:50.120 --> 02:51.010
And just like that

02:51.010 --> 02:54.760
we have found four zeros
for this polynomial,

02:54.760 --> 02:57.290
when x equals one the
polynomial's equal to zero,

02:57.290 --> 02:59.580
when x equals negative two the
polynomial's equal to zero,

02:59.580 --> 03:02.020
when x equals three the
polynomial's equal to zero,

03:02.020 --> 03:03.730
and when x equals negative four

03:03.730 --> 03:05.480
the polynomial's equal to zero.

03:05.480 --> 03:07.030
And one of the interesting things

03:07.030 --> 03:09.230
about the zeros of a polynomial

03:09.230 --> 03:11.370
you could actually use
that to start to sketch out

03:11.370 --> 03:13.470
what the graph might look like.

03:13.470 --> 03:16.550
So, for example, we know,
that this polynomial

03:16.550 --> 03:21.190
is going to take on the
value zero at these zeros.

03:21.190 --> 03:23.320
So let me just draw a rough
sketch right over here.

03:23.320 --> 03:28.250
So this is my x-axis, that's
my y-axis, that's my y-axis.

03:28.250 --> 03:31.050
And so, let's see, at x equals one,

03:31.050 --> 03:31.940
so let me just do it this way,

03:31.940 --> 03:36.920
so we have one, two, three, and four.

03:36.920 --> 03:41.797
Then you have negative one,
negative two, negative three,

03:41.797 --> 03:44.439
and then last but not
least, negative four.

03:44.439 --> 03:47.470
We know that this polynomial, p of x,

03:47.470 --> 03:50.190
is going to be equal to
zero at x equals one.

03:50.190 --> 03:53.100
So it's going to intersect
the x-axis right there.

03:53.100 --> 03:56.470
It's going to be equal to
zero at x equals negative two,

03:56.470 --> 03:58.023
so right over there.

03:58.023 --> 04:00.840
At x equals three, right over there.

04:00.840 --> 04:02.800
And x equals negative four.

04:02.800 --> 04:05.150
Now we don't know exactly
what the graph looks like,

04:05.150 --> 04:06.150
just based on this.

04:06.150 --> 04:08.690
We could try out some values
on either side to figure out,

04:08.690 --> 04:11.570
hey, is it above the
x-axis, or below the x-axis,

04:11.570 --> 04:13.427
for x-values less than negative four.

04:13.427 --> 04:15.060
And we can try things out like that

04:15.060 --> 04:18.290
but we know it intersects
the x-axis at these points.

04:18.290 --> 04:21.270
So it might look something like this,

04:21.270 --> 04:22.947
this is a very rough sketch.

04:22.947 --> 04:26.930
It might look something like this,

04:26.930 --> 04:30.340
we don't know without doing
a little bit more work.

04:30.340 --> 04:32.920
But ahead of time, I took a
look at what this looks like,

04:32.920 --> 04:34.850
I went on to Desmos, and I graphed it,

04:34.850 --> 04:39.670
and you can see, it looks
exactly as what we would expect.

04:39.670 --> 04:43.160
The graph of this polynomial
intersects the x-axis

04:43.160 --> 04:45.930
at x equals negative four,
actually let me color code it,

04:45.930 --> 04:47.710
x equals negative four,

04:47.710 --> 04:50.280
and that is that zero right over there,

04:50.280 --> 04:54.588
x equals negative two,
that's this zero right there,

04:54.588 --> 04:58.380
x equals one, right over there,

04:58.380 --> 05:03.380
and then x equals three, right over there.

05:03.492 --> 05:06.923
In future videos we will
study this in even more depth.
